WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.366
Let's take a look at how
to add bleed in Canva.

00:00:04.366 --> 00:00:10.090
So the bleed in Canva is set to a
production standard, but you can add it.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.870
We don't have any bleed on this document
right now. You can't even see it.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:18.590
But if we go to File, down to View
Settings, we can see Show Print Bleed.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.990
So if we click on this, you can
see that the bleed has been added.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:29.190
There's a dotted line around our original
size artwork and then the extra bleed space.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:34.110
Now you may have to increase your background
size to make sure you fit that in case there's

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:38.630
elements that don't go all the way,
stretch all the way outside of the bleed.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:43.750
So if we click on one of these, let's see what we
get. So that was an example for a moment there.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.870
But I think because it's a colored
background, it went ahead and extended it.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.790
What we need is something that has a photo background.
So I think this one's a good example here.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:57.270
Yeah, so this has actually a photo in the
background and that photo doesn't extend

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:04.470
the edge. You can see some of this blue area here.
That means that this will not actually export properly.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.710
So in this case, I would want to double
click on that photo background and make

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:14.030
sure I extend it past the canvas or move
it to make sure that it extends past the

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.870
bleed in my document. And once I've done that, I
could click off of the photo and you can see how it

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.870
extends all the way past the bleed,
all the way around this design.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:30.270
Now that you have that, that's when you
would go to share, down to download, and

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.990
then you would select PDF print, which
would be the highest quality print setting.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.950
And you have to make sure to
select crop marks and bleed.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:43.270
You want to include that in the PDF if
you're actually exporting with bleed.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.830
From there, of course, if you're sending
it to a printer, they probably want it

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:52.030
in CMYK, which is a Canva Pro feature. But otherwise, if
you're printing it for yourself with some bleed, you can just

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:57.430
select RGB if you really want, and then download.
And that will include the bleed in your document.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:01.830
And you can see where that bleed is
here and then stretch your graphics or

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:06.590
scale them around just to make sure
they cover and go off the page if you

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.110
have graphics that go outside of the
size, or if you have graphics that go

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.670
to the edge of your document,
that's what the bleed is there for.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:20.630
So they can trim it off and you actually have a nice
clean design. That's how you can add bleed here in Canva.

